Resolve the CSS-wide keywords on every property and shorthand (CSS Cascade 4): a declaration whose whole value is
inherit, initial, unset, revert or revert-layer (revert in a cascade without layers) parsed as invalid and was dropped,
so a lower declaration such as the user-agent `select { color }` stood where a page's `select { color: inherit }` reads
its container's.
unset is inherit or initial as the property inherits. An inherited property's initial is its initial value; that of a
property that does not inherit reads as no declaration, as its initial value does where layout tells an undeclared
property (an automatic minimum width) from a declared one. revert is the declaration the user-agent origin's rules alone
give the element (StyleSheetContainer::SetUserAgentOrigin marks a sheet's rules as that origin), or none: an element
definition resolves a revert winning among its rules, the cascade a local one.
An inherited property's inherit is its parent's computed value already. One that does not inherit takes the declaration
giving the parent its value (an inherit there defers further up; the initial value where the parent declares none),
computed in the declaring element's font size, zoom and viewport; vertical-align and the border widths, whose computed
values read other properties of their element, copy the parent's computed value. The zoom factor, registered as the
inherited effective zoom, takes the parent's own. Specified-value readers (GetProperty, the local-property reads of
layout and ComputedValues, transitions) see that declaration, and a child declaring inherit on a property that does not
inherit is dirtied by its parent's change of it, the parent counting such children to skip the walk without them.
A keyframe's keyword resolves as its animation starts: initial to the initial value, revert to the element's user-agent
declaration, inherit (and revert without one on an inherited property) to the declaration giving the parent its value,
its lengths computed where it is declared.
A shorthand's keyword sets every longhand, with the ones its value syntax here leaves out: line-height and font-kerning
for font, the border-image longhands for border; removing the shorthand removes them too.
PropertySpecification::ParseCssWideKeyword names the keyword a value is.

